+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Privilege elevation
===================

VeraCrypt runs its privileged core service through doas(1).  Add a rule
to /etc/doas.conf before mounting, creating or formatting volumes:

    permit :wheel cmd ${TRUEPREFIX}/bin/veracrypt args --core-service-no-fork

This rule permits only the privileged core service used internally by
VeraCrypt. Run the normal CLI or GUI as your own user. Do not use
"permit nopass" unless you accept passwordless elevation for every user
allowed by the rule. Membership in the group named by the rule is
effectively root-equivalent: anyone who can run the core service can ask
it to mount, unmount and format volumes. Keep the rule narrow and review
it after every upgrade.

Version 1.26.31 uses --core-service-no-fork for doas elevation. Replace the
older --core-service argument in existing rules when upgrading. The port
uses doas even if sudo is also installed.

The core service is used for operations that require root privileges,
such as configuring vnd(4) loop devices, mounting and unmounting
filesystems, changing ownership of device nodes, and running newfs(8)
when an OpenBSD FFS filesystem is created inside a volume. The ownership
request accepts only character or block device nodes directly under
/dev; it cannot chown an arbitrary file or follow a symbolic link outside
that directory. It does not limit which raw device under /dev may be
transferred, so the doas rule must still be restricted to trusted users.

Filesystem creation
===================

OpenBSD FFS filesystems can be created inside a new volume by selecting
the "FFS" filesystem in the volume creation wizard or by passing
"--filesystem=FFS" on the command line.  The filesystem is created with
newfs(8) on the raw vnd(4) character device through the elevated core
service. Changing device ownership alone is insufficient: reading the
disk label requires privileged ioctls on OpenBSD.

Noninteractive creation retains FAT as its default. Request FFS
explicitly with --filesystem=FFS. Without a passwordless doas rule, use
interactive mode so VeraCrypt can request your account password.
